Floating point aliases named by width, as the integers are: `ca::f32` is `float` and
`ca::f64` is `double`. Each header checks at compile time that the type really is IEEE 754
binary32 or binary64 (its width in bits and its mantissa digits), so code
that relies on the bit layout, such as the shortest float formatting of layer 3, fails to build
on a target where it would be wrong.

```c++
#include "ca/bit.hpp"
#include "ca/floats.hpp"
#include "ca/integers.hpp"
#include "ca/limits.hpp"

static_assert(sizeof(ca::f64) == 8U);
static_assert(ca::bit_cast<ca::u64>(ca::f64{-0.0}) == 0x8000'0000'0000'0000U);
static_assert(ca::limits<ca::f32>::infinity() > ca::limits<ca::f32>::max());

[[nodiscard]] constexpr auto is_nan(const ca::f64 value) noexcept -> bool {
	return value != value;
}

static_assert(is_nan(ca::limits<ca::f64>::quiet_nan()));
```

`long double` has no alias: its layout differs between targets (80-bit x87 on x86-64,
binary128 or binary64 elsewhere), and nothing in ca depends on it.
